  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100869,
      "id": "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140",
      "title": "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8c1748bb-90d6-5563-8ef1-b046b936907d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nO=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -9 & -4 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $O,P,$ and $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The product $OR$ is well defined\n\n- **II.** The product $OP$ is well defined\n\n- **III.** The product $RO$ is well defined",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $O$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector, $P$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, and $R$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector.\n\nThere are $5$ columns in $O$ and $3$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $O$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $O$ and $R$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{O}_{1\\times 5}{\\times }_{\\ne }{R}_{3\\times 1}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $5$ columns in $O$ and $4$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $O$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $O$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{O}_{1\\times 5}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{4\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there is $1$ column in $R$ and $1$ row in $O.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ equals the number of rows in $O,$ the matrices $R$ and $O$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{O}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 5}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement III is true, while statements I and II are false.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
